Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.1.1", "inferred_role": "corporate_edge_router_main", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:08:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-02-04T02:24:42+0100", "agent_name": "CORP-EDGE-RTR-01"},
    {"ip": "91.219.236.87", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_router_admin_source", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:08:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-02-04T02:24:42+0100"},
    {"ip": "45.95.169.142", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_gre_c2_destination", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:15:18+0100", "last_seen": "2028-02-04T02:24:42+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts (Network Device)", "technique": "T1078 + T1556", "what_happened": "At 2028-02-04 02:08:42 CEST, the edge router CORP-EDGE-RTR-01 (10.30.1.1) emitted a syslog %SYS-5-CONFIG_I message indicating 'Configured from console by admin from 91.219.236.87 via vty0' — i.e., the built-in 'admin' user successfully authenticated to the router CLI (vty0 = first VTY/SSH/Telnet line) FROM AN EXTERNAL PUBLIC IP. Corporate policy mandates that all network-device administration must originate from the NOC management subnet (10.30.250.0/24) over the corporate VPN — direct CLI access from the public internet to the edge router is STRICTLY FORBIDDEN. The fact that auth succeeded from an external IP means either (a) the router admin credentials were leaked/phished/brute-forced, or (b) an ACL exception was illegitimately added permitting external CLI access, or (c) both. The router is the ROOT OF NETWORK TRUST — compromise of the edge router is equivalent to compromise of the entire corporate egress/ingress path.", "chain_reason": "Cisco IOS syslog facility SYS, severity 5, mnemonic CONFIG_I, forwarded to Wazuh via the network-device syslog collector and decoded under data.router.audit. The message format 'Configured from console by <user> from <ip> via <line>' is the canonical Cisco config-change marker. Linkage: agent.name=CORP-EDGE-RTR-01 + data.router.audit message contains 'admin' + '91.219.236.87' + 'vty0'.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:08:42+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Impair Defenses (Wazuh Decoder Alert)", "technique": "T1562", "what_happened": "At 02:11:18 CEST, the Wazuh router-audit decoder fired a HIGH-severity alert: 'Router admin login from non-corp/non-VPN external IP — should not happen on edge router CORP-EDGE-RTR-01'. The decoder cross-references the source IP against the NOC management subnet (10.30.250.0/24) + corporate VPN concentrator egress IPs; 91.219.236.87 matches neither. This is a definitive integrity-of-network-device-management violation. It is the EARLIEST point where the SOC could have caught this in real time.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh local_rule cross-correlates data.router.audit (admin login source IP) against an allowlist of corporate NOC + VPN IPs. Linkage: rule fires when agent.name matches a network-device hostname pattern AND the parsed source-IP field falls outside the NOC/VPN allowlist.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:11:18+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Modify Authentication Process (Network Device)", "technique": "T1556 + T1098", "what_happened": "At 02:14:42 CEST, the router emitted '%SEC-6-IPACCESSLOGP: list WAN-IN permitted tcp 91.219.236.87(443) -> 10.30.5.0/24 (0)' — i.e., the WAN-IN ingress ACL on the public-facing interface now PERMITS inbound TCP from 91.219.236.87 to the entire 10.30.5.0/24 internal segment. This ACL rule did NOT exist 7 minutes earlier. The attacker, having authenticated to the router CLI in step 1, added an inbound-permit rule to give themselves persistent network reachability INTO the corporate internal network from their external IP — bypassing the perimeter firewall entirely. This is a network-device-level persistence touch: even if the attacker loses their CLI session, the ACL rule remains and lets them reconnect.", "chain_reason": "Cisco IOS syslog facility SEC, severity 6, mnemonic IPACCESSLOGP. The IPACCESSLOGP message is emitted each time the named ACL ('WAN-IN') matches a permitted packet — its appearance on a NEW src_ip → dest range pair is the diagnostic of a NEW ACL rule. Linkage: agent.name=CORP-EDGE-RTR-01 + data.router.audit message matches 'list WAN-IN permitted' + '91.219.236.87' + '10.30.5.0/24'.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:14:42+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Collection / Adversary-in-the-Middle (Traffic Mirror)", "technique": "T1557 + T1020.001", "what_happened": "At 02:15:18 CEST, the router emitted '%TUNNEL-5-UP: Tunnel0 (GRE) source 10.30.1.1 destination 45.95.169.142' — a NEW GRE (Generic Routing Encapsulation) tunnel just came up, with the router itself as source and an external public IP 45.95.169.142 as destination. GRE tunnels are commonly used in legitimate WAN topologies (site-to-site links to known partner ASNs / corporate branches), but the destination 45.95.169.142 is NOT in the corporate inter-site IP allowlist + the tunnel was created without a change ticket. The attacker is building an exfiltration covert channel: traffic mirroring (SPAN-style) of corporate egress traffic can be redirected through Tunnel0 to the attacker C2, giving them passive visibility into ALL outbound corporate traffic in real time.", "chain_reason": "Cisco IOS syslog facility TUNNEL, severity 5, mnemonic UP. The format 'Tunnel<N> (GRE) source <internal_ip> destination <external_ip>' is the canonical tunnel-establishment marker. Linkage: agent.name=CORP-EDGE-RTR-01 + data.router.audit message matches 'TUNNEL-5-UP' + 'Tunnel0' + '45.95.169.142'.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:15:18+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Exfiltration / Exfiltration Over C2 Channel", "technique": "T1041 + T1020", "what_happened": "At 02:18:42 CEST, the Suricata netflow log showed external destination 45.95.169.142 receiving 1.2 GB/min sustained outbound flow from internal source ranges via the new Tunnel0 GRE tunnel. The volume + destination + tunnel-correlation pattern is the textbook covert-egress signature: passive corporate egress traffic is being SPAN-mirrored through the GRE tunnel to the attacker C2 in near-real-time. 1.2 GB/min = ~20 MB/sec sustained = far above any legitimate inter-site replication baseline for this router. The attacker now has streaming visibility into every cleartext or TLS-protected (still observable: SNI, IP, timing, size) outbound corporate connection — email, SaaS, API calls, DNS, the works.", "chain_reason": "Suricata netflow log (data.src_ip / data.dest_ip / flow.bytes_toserver). Linkage: data.dest_ip=45.95.169.142 + flow.proto=gre + bytes >> baseline. Cross-correlated with step 4's tunnel establishment by destination-IP equality.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:18:42+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Indicator Removal (ACL Auditing Gap)", "technique": "T1070 + T1562.006", "what_happened": "At 02:24:42 CEST, the router emitted '%LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: ACL WAN-IN modified — line count changed from 247 to 251' — i.e., the WAN-IN ACL grew by 4 rules in the past 16 minutes. NCM (Network Configuration Management) tooling normally requires an ITSM change ticket linked to every ACL modification; the SOC's ITSM lookup at investigation time returned ZERO open or recently-closed change tickets affecting CORP-EDGE-RTR-01's WAN-IN ACL in this window. The 4 new rules are: (1) the 02:14:42 permit-attacker-IP-to-internal rule observed in step 3, plus 3 additional unobserved rules that warrant URGENT diff against the last-known-good baseline. ACL modifications without change-ticket linkage are themselves diagnostic of unauthorized configuration tampering.", "chain_reason": "Cisco IOS syslog facility LINEPROTO, severity 5, mnemonic UPDOWN. The ACL-line-count delta is computed by Wazuh's NCM-integrated decoder by snapshot-diffing the router config every 60 seconds. Linkage: agent.name=CORP-EDGE-RTR-01 + data.router.audit message matches 'ACL WAN-IN modified' + line-count delta != 0 + NO matching ITSM change ticket.", "first_seen": "2028-02-04T02:24:42+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2028-02-04T02:08:42+0100", "end": "2028-02-04T02:24:42+0100"},
